zeroMemory: do not need memset_explicit() from the C library on glibc

Upstream: reverts platform/system/libhwbinder commit efd89fe
  "Use our bionic and glibc memset_explicit() polyfill." (Elliott Hughes,
  2025-06-06, Bug 416248344), for this build only.

Why: that commit expects a memset_explicit() on glibc hosts. The polyfill is not
in any project this build fetches, and glibc 2.31 from our Debian 11 sysroot has
none, so the file does not compile. The code here is the form the commit
removed, which is also what
frameworks/native/libs/binder/Utils.h still does at this tag on non-bionic
hosts: memset followed by a compiler barrier. The official library imports no
memset_explicit either.

--- a/Utils.h
+++ b/Utils.h
@@ -22,7 +22,10 @@
 namespace android::hardware {
 
 inline void zeroMemory(uint8_t* data, size_t size) {
-    memset_explicit(data, 0, size);
+    memset(data, 0, size);
+    // Assembly marking to prevent any optimizing compiler from not actually clearing the buffer,
+    // this matches what exactly what memset_explicit does.
+    __asm__ __volatile__("" : : "r"(data) : "memory");
 }
 
 }   // namespace android::hardware
